<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latestMajor</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    IN-1 and phase 2: no model and no storage. Nothing here calls an LLM and nothing here
    writes anything; a file goes in and a description of what is in it comes out, the same way
    every time.

    The contract is referenced for one thing: ColumnType. Inference has to conclude in the
    vocabulary the storage can actually hold, and a second enum here would be two vocabularies
    to keep in step for no gain. The contract assembly depends on nothing itself, so this
    borrows no weight by taking it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\TokkDb.Assistant.Storage\TokkDb.Assistant.Storage.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      R-5, settled. DocumentFormat.OpenXml over ClosedXML, ExcelDataReader and MiniExcel, for
      four reasons, in order of weight:

      1. It reads .docx as well, and 2.2 needs that. Every alternative here is spreadsheets
         only, so choosing one means taking this as well - and ClosedXML already depends on
         this, so choosing it means taking both plus a DOM.
      2. It streams. OpenXmlReader walks a sheet part forward without holding it, so the
         50 000-row file of IN-3 is read a row at a time. ClosedXML's XLWorkbook is a document
         model and loads the workbook; a desktop application that reads 50 MB to produce a 2 KB
         rendering is paying for nothing.
      3. Microsoft, MIT, .NET Foundation, 431 million downloads. There is no question about it
         still being here in a year.
      4. It is what the format actually is, so nothing is hidden: shared strings, number
         formats and the 1900 date system are visible rather than guessed at by a layer that
         may guess differently from the next version of itself.

      The cost R-5 names is real and is not waved away: shared strings, styles and date formats
      have to be read by hand. That work is in Xlsx.cs and nowhere else.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="DocumentFormat.OpenXml" Version="3.5.1" />

    <!-- The single-byte code pages a file with no byte order mark might be in. They ship with
         .NET 10 rather than needing a package; CodePagesEncodingProvider still has to be
         registered before they can be asked for. -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="TokkDb.Assistant.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
